```

Embedding can fetch one aggregate in one read, but it may duplicate data or create a growing document. It does not by itself make horizontal scaling easy; partition-key choice, indexes, document size, and update patterns still matter.

```sql
-- Multi-table consistency in one ACID transaction: the relational sweet spot
START TRANSACTION;
UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 100 WHERE id = 1;
UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + 100 WHERE id = 2;
INSERT INTO transfers (from_id, to_id, amount) VALUES (1, 2, 100);
COMMIT;  -- all three changes commit together or none do
```

Choose relational when you need joins across well-defined entities and transactional guarantees across multiple rows or tables.

```json
{
  "_id": 1,
  "name": "Asha",
  "orders": [
    { "total": 250.00 },
    { "total": 80.50 }
  ]
}
```

Embedding can read one aggregate without a join, but it may duplicate data or create a growing document. It does not make sharding automatic; document size, indexes, shard key, and update patterns still matter.

```javascript
// MongoDB: fetch a document, related data already embedded
db.users.findOne({ _id: 1 })
// -> { _id: 1, name: 'Asha', orders: [ { total: 250.0 } ] }
```

```sql
-- MySQL: the same result requires a join across normalized tables
SELECT u.name, o.total
FROM users u JOIN orders o ON o.user_id = u.id
WHERE u.id = 1;
```

MongoDB favors embedding for read speed and flexible structure; MySQL favors normalized tables with joins and strong integrity.

```java
@Service
public class ProductService {
    private final ProductRepository products;
    private final RedisTemplate<String, Product> redis;

    public Product getProduct(long productId) {
        String key = "product:" + productId;
        Product cached = redis.opsForValue().get(key);
        if (cached != null) {
            return cached;
        }

        Product product = products.findById(productId).orElseThrow();
        redis.opsForValue().set(key, product, Duration.ofMinutes(5));
        return product;
    }
}
```

This is cache-aside, not a latency guarantee. A production design also handles invalidation, concurrent misses, serialization, timeouts, and Redis failure. For a fixed-window rate limit, set the count and expiry atomically—commonly with a Lua script—instead of separate `INCR` and `EXPIRE` calls.

```javascript
// Both shapes are valid in the same collection when no validator forbids them.
db.users.insertMany([
  { _id: "u1", name: "Alice", email: "alice@example.com" },
  { _id: "u2", name: "Bob", email: "bob@example.com", phoneNumber: "555-1234" }
]);

// A reader must handle the optional field.
db.users.find(
  {},
  { name: 1, phoneNumber: 1 }
);
```

A collection validator can still require `_id`, `name`, and `email` to have specific BSON types while leaving `phoneNumber` optional.

